  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Oakland Athletics stumbled out of the gate and never truly found their footing, finishing with a 64-98 record that told the story of a franchise still searching for its identity. From the very first game, the season was defined by a maddening push-and-pull: moments of genuine promise quickly erased by pitching collapses and untimely defensive lapses. Shea Langeliers gave fans something to cheer about on Opening Day, but the bullpen couldn't hold leads and the rotation leaked runs at an alarming rate, setting the tone for a long summer.\n\nThe spring months offered tantalizing glimpses of what the Athletics could be. Nick Kurtz emerged as a legitimate force, driving in runs and flashing the kind of power that had scouts excited. Young players like Jacob Wilson and Tyler Soderstrom showed flashes of their potential, and a few timely wins kept the season from feeling completely hopeless through April and May. But for every encouraging sequence, an opposing hitter would launch a crushing home run off an overmatched reliever, and the losses piled up faster than the wins.\n\nThe summer became a grind of inconsistency. The pitching staff — burdened by a 5.51 ERA — surrendered 937 runs over the course of the season, making nearly every lead feel fragile. Relievers like Elvis Alvarado and Justin Sterner found themselves on both sides of the ledger, saving games one week and surrendering devastating home runs the next. By late June, playoff contention was a distant dream, and the organization shifted its focus toward evaluating young talent and building toward the future.\n\nThe final stretch of the 2026 season was a sobering meditation on the gap between potential and results. Lawrence Butler's bat showed power — including a brutal walk-off home run that hurt his own team in a September collapse — and Carlos Cortes provided some late-season theatrics, but the losses kept coming. Oakland's 194 home runs suggested offensive firepower existed, but a rotation that couldn't keep games close and a bullpen that crumbled under pressure meant the Athletics would watch October baseball from home once again, left to wonder what another offseason of development might bring.",
